1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,350
Recommendation feeds learn us over years,
then make us start over

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,700
on every new account. Curate turns that history
into something portable.

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:13,500
I can speak naturally: “I want less ragebait
and more science-based pages.”

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,800
I give Curate a few posts from my feed.
It reads only what I chose,

5
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:23,700
separates evidence from inference,
and builds a mix I can inspect.

6
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:30,500
It also understands precision. Here I ask for fifty
percent astronomy, fifteen percent coding,

7
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,300
twelve percent drawing, plus anime, Naruto,
One Piece, and perfumes.

8
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:39,900
The request stays a sentence; the agent turns it into
a bounded plan for the selected app.

9
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:46,200
Then Curate runs the plan on a safe practice feed.
The result is visible: outrage and unsupported claims

10
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:52,600
move out, while astronomy, programming, research,
drawing, anime analysis, and fragrance chemistry move in.

11
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:57,350
Finally, Curate restores the starting state
and verifies the match.

12
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:02,890
Natural language in, a measurable feed out,
and a return route if I change my mind.
